<biblStruct xml:id="b10">
	<monogr>
		<title level="m" type="main">These developments are particularly relevant for web applications where data is often imbalanced, such as in recommendation systems or content moderation platforms. In this tutorial, we will first provide a brief overview of the core challenges associated with long-tailed learning and its various downstream applications in web contexts. For instance, we&apos;ll discuss how long-tailed learning can enhance user experience by ensuring that niche products are recommended alongside popular ones in e-commerce settings. Next, we will review classical methods for addressing long-tailed learning, highlighting exemplar approaches from the perspectives of data augmentation, model ensemble, and loss design. This includes practical examples such as augmenting minority class data in social media analysis to improve sentiment detection or employing model ensembles to boost the accuracy of classifiers used in web search algorithms. Finally, we will explore future directions for long-tailed learning, particularly in weakly-supervised and self-supervised settings, which are increasingly relevant for web applications where labeled data may be scarce. By summarizing these developments</title>
		<author>
			<persName><forename type="first">Iii (jiangchao</forename><surname>Presenter</surname></persName>
		</author>
		<author>
			<persName><surname>Yao</surname></persName>
		</author>
		<imprint/>
	</monogr>
	<note>Duration: 80 minutes Title: Trustworthy Learning under Long-tailed Web Data Abstract In recent years, long-tailed learning has gained renewed attention in the field of Web AI, particularly with advancements in representation learning, model ensemble techniques, and loss design we aim to help attendees systematically understand the past, present, and future of long-tailed learning in the context of Web AI, ultimately empowering them to implement more effective solutions in their own applications. Relevant papers by the presenter</note>
</biblStruct>

